task: dataflow-once
agent: opus-dataflow
started: 2026-09-10
branch: feature/dataflow-once
worktree: ../okay-wt-dfo1
spec: specs/dataflow.md, stage 6c

why: five stages of recovery all rest on the same trade — a partition
  may be COMPUTED twice, and that is correct because the coordinator
  keeps one partial per partition. But nothing has ever left the
  engine. `Run[R]` is a value in memory, and the first time a result
  is WRITTEN somewhere the question changes from "is the arithmetic
  right" to "did this row land twice".

what: the sink seam, and the identity that makes a repeat harmless.

  A retired pane already HAS an identity — (window start, key) — and
  it is unique by construction, because a window is a half-open
  interval and a key is a key. So a keyed write is exactly-once by
  construction rather than by protocol: there is no transaction, no
  two-phase commit, no dedup table, and nothing to tune.

  `Sink.tumblingTo` / `slidingTo`: a windowed sink whose retired panes
  are handed to a writer WITH that identity, answering how many were
  written. The engine's promise is then precisely statable: every
  (window, key) is offered exactly once per run, in no particular
  order, and a writer that is keyed makes that an exactly-once
  OUTCOME.

  The test injects worker deaths and asserts the store holds each key
  exactly once with the batch run's values — so the claim is checked
  under the conditions that would break it, not on a quiet run.

what this is NOT, and the boundary is sharp: exactly-once ACROSS RUNS.
  A coordinator that dies and restarts re-offers everything, because
  it journals nothing. Within a run the identity is enough; across
  runs it needs the journal, which is `dataflow-coordinator`.

predict: the mechanism is small and the SPEC sentence is the hard
  part — "exactly once" is the most over-claimed phrase in this
  field, and the honest version has two qualifiers in it.
